    "q-344403": {
      "dom_id": "step-q344403",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=\\frac{x-1}{\\sqrt{x-4}}$ have a local minimum? Do not round the answer.\n\nThe local minimum is at $x=$\n{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-4}}+(x-1)(-\\frac{1}{2})(x-4)^{-3/2} \\\\  & =\\,\\frac{x-7}{2(x-4)^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-7}{2(x-4)^{3/2}} & =0 \\\\ x-7 & =0 \\\\ x & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=7$ is a stationary point. Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=5,8$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(5) & =\\frac{5-7}{2(5-4)^{3/2}}=\\frac{-2}{2(1)^{3/2}}=-1<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(8) & =\\frac{8-7}{2(8-4)^{3/2}}=\\frac{1}{2(4)^{3/2}}=\\frac{1}{16}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(4,7)$ | $7$ | $(7,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $5$ |  | $8$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\n- At $x=7,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=7$ is a local minimum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local minimum at $x=7.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
